# websim byte-fidelity policy
# =============================================================================
# This port's entire premise is byte-identity against a certified Java model.
# Fixture files under engine/test/fixtures/, pipeline/java-exporter/fixtures/
# and validation/ are carried with SHA-256 provenance digests (see e.g.
# engine/test/fixtures/graph-slice/provenance.json and the corpus digest suite
# at engine/test/determinism/corpus.digest.test.ts). Those digests were computed
# over the bytes as dumped by the Java exporters, which are LF-terminated.
#
# This repository is developed on Windows, where git's default `core.autocrlf`
# is `true`. Without the rule below, git rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout, so a
# fresh clone would materialise fixture bytes that differ from the ones the
# digests were taken over -- every provenance assertion would fail, and it would
# fail only on a clean clone, i.e. exactly in CI and never on the machine that
# wrote the files. That is the same "green locally, red on first push" failure
# class the WP7 remediation pass was opened to eliminate.
#
# `-text` disables all end-of-line conversion: git stores and restores these
# bytes verbatim on every platform. It is deliberately applied to the WHOLE
# subtree rather than to a fixture allow-list, because a new dumper adding a new
# fixture directory must not be able to reintroduce the defect by omission.
#
# Consequence to be aware of: source files here keep LF on Windows checkouts.
# That is intended. Determinism outranks editor convention in this tree.
* -text

# Binary assets: also marked explicitly so git never attempts a textual diff or
# merge on them, independent of the blanket rule above.
*.bin binary
*.br binary
*.gz binary
*.zip binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary
